==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|orogénie}}, from {{uder|en|grc|ὄρος||mountain, high ground}} + {{m|grc|γενεια||creation, birth, making}}. Surface etymology is {{confix|en|oro|geny}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɒˈɹɒdʒɪni/|/əˈɹɒdʒɪni/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|geology}} The [[process]] of [[mountain]] [[building]] by the [[upward]] [[folding]] of the [[Earth]]'s [[crust]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=P. A. Cawood|chapter=Acadian '''orogeny''' in west Newfoundland: Definition, character, and significance|editors=David C. Roy; James William Skehan|title=The Acadian '''Orogeny''': Recent Studies in New England|publisher=w:Geological Society of America|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=PU_RpfZ_2YgC&pg=PA138&dq=%22orogeny%22%7C%22orogenies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiqj9mItrrXAhXnyVQKHUieDFMQ6AEIMDAC#v=onepage&q=%22orogeny%22%7C%22orogenies%22&f=false|page=138
|passage=The effects of Acadian '''orogeny''' are concentrated in central Newfoundland, decreasing in intensity to the east and west (Williams, this volume).}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Richard Fortey|title=The Earth|page=102|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2011
|passage=When I asked Geoff Milnes what age the rocks were ''before'' they were turned into uncompromising gneisses by the Alpine '''orogeny''' he made a rueful face.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Robert S. Hildebrand|title=Did Westward Subduction Cause Cretaceous-Tertiary '''Orogeny''' in the North American Cordillera?|publisher=w:Geological Society of America|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=BNm12eLbQA0C&pg=PA5&dq=%22orogeny%22%7C%22orogenies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiqj9mItrrXAhXnyVQKHUieDFMQ6AEINDAD#v=onepage&q=%22orogeny%22%7C%22orogenies%22&f=false|page=5
|passage=In Canada the fold-thrust belt formed during what is termed the Columbian '''orogeny''' (Monger and Price, 2002). Because the Sevier and Columbian '''orogenies''' were contemporaneous and constitute a single continuous and linear belt of rocks and structures, I use the term Cordillera '''orogeny''' to refer to the Cretaceous deformation that created the entire belt, whereas the term Laramide is used only to refer to a series of Late Cretaceous—Tertiary basement-involved uplifts, for the most part located in the Rocky Mountain foreland east of the fold-thrust belt and mainly south of the Lewis and Clark lineament (Fig. 1) in keeping with the original definition by Armstrong (1968).}}

